A viable family has emerged, but it needs an honest Track B label. I’m using native \(P_3\)-free Edge Deletion (cluster deletion): the generated graph is the line graph of a regular bipartite incidence graph, and either star-clique factor gives a deletion certificate. The certificate is compressed as a 2-dimensional finite-field subspace whose cosets specify the retained clusters. A generic cluster-deletion search is exponential in \(k\), while a line-graph reconstruction is linear on this promised distribution; the intended shortcut is to notice that maximal-clique coordinate differences share a span. I’ll now implement and measure that gap rather than claim the paper’s worst-case theorem applies distributionally.
